    "id": "p51-q23-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "23",
    "prompt": "True or False? Let $A$, $B$ and $C$ be sets in a universal set $U$.\n\n$(A\\cap B)\\cap C=A\\cap(B\\cup C)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "False.",
      "worked": "False. The left side requires membership in all three sets; the right requires membership in $A$ and at least one of $B$ or $C$. For $U=\\{1\\}$, $A=B=\\{1\\}$ and $C=\\varnothing$,\n$$\\begin{align*}(A\\cap B)\\cap C&=\\varnothing,\\\\A\\cap(B\\cup C)&=\\{1\\}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 6
